    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve the equation \\((x! - (x-3)!) \\div 23 = 1\\), we start by simplifying the expression inside the parentheses. The equation can be rewritten as:\n\n\\[ \\frac{x! - (x-3)!}{23} = 1 \\]\n\nMultiplying both sides by 23, we get:\n\n\\[ x! - (x-3)! = 23 \\]\n\nNext, we need to find a value of \\(x\\) that satisfies this equation. Let's start by testing small positive integer values for \\(x\\).\n\n1. For \\(x = 1\\):\n   \\[ 1! - (1-3)! = 1 - 2! = 1 - 2 = -1 \\]\n   \\[ -1 \\neq 23 \\]\n   So, \\(x = 1\\) is not a solution.\n\n2. For \\(x = 2\\):\n   \\[ 2! - (2-3)! = 2 - 1! = 2 - 1 = 1 \\]\n   \\[ 1 \\neq 23 \\]\n   So, \\(x = 2\\) is not a solution.\n\n3. For \\(x = 3\\):\n   \\[ 3! - (3-3)! = 6 - 0! = 6 - 1 = 5 \\]\n   \\[ 5 \\neq 23 \\]\n   So, \\(x = 3\\) is not a solution.\n\n4. For \\(x = 4\\):\n   \\[ 4! - (4-3)! = 24 - 1! = 24 - 1 = 23 \\]\n   \\[ 23 = 23 \\]\n   So, \\(x = 4\\) is a solution.\n\nWe can verify that \\(x = 4\\) is indeed the solution by substituting it back into the original equation:\n\n\\[ \\frac{4! - (4-3)!}{23} = \\frac{24 - 1}{23} = \\frac{23}{23} = 1 \\]\n\nSince the equation holds true for \\(x = 4\\), the value of \\(x\\) is \\(\\boxed{4}\\).",
